{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 San Diego Padres entered the season with championship aspirations, and their April surge — going 14-8 — announced them as legitimate contenders. The rotation was sharp, the bullpen was mostly trustworthy, and the lineup showed flashes of the explosive offense San Diego needed. But even in that strong opening month, the cracks were visible: closer Taylor Rogers was already showing signs of inconsistency, and the bullpen's vulnerability in high-leverage spots would become a defining story of the season.\n\nMay and June brought both resilience and turbulence. Jorge Alfaro delivered one of the season's most electric moments with a three-run walk-off homer in early May, and Ha-Seong Kim's clutch hitting provided repeated sparks. But Taylor Rogers was becoming a genuine liability, surrendering crushing home runs and extra-base hits in save situations with alarming regularity. The Padres stayed afloat through the summer — going 16-11 in May and 16-13 in June — largely on the strength of their pitching staff and timely contributions from unlikely heroes like Luke Voit and Wil Myers.\n\nJuly was the Padres' one real stumble, a 11-14 record that coincided with the Rogers implosion reaching its peak. Back-to-back gut-punch losses in late July, including a brutal walk-off double off Rogers on consecutive days, pushed San Diego to a crossroads. The front office responded aggressively at the trade deadline, acquiring Juan Soto and Josh Bell in a blockbuster deal that reshaped the franchise's identity. August's 16-13 record confirmed the Padres had stabilized, with Brandon Drury's three-run homer and Trent Grisham's clutch power providing momentum into September's playoff push.\n\nThe postseason was the reward for all that perseverance. San Diego shocked the baseball world by eliminating the 111-win Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLDS, with Jake Cronenworth and Ha-Seong Kim delivering in the clinching moments. But the Philadelphia Phillies proved a bridge too far in the NLCS, with Bryce Harper's two-run homer off Robert Suarez in Game 5 delivering a devastating blow that encapsulated the series. The Padres' October run — their deepest since 1998 — ended in heartbreak, but it confirmed that this franchise had genuinely arrived on the national stage.",
